@startuml 03-sequence-immediate
' =====================================================================
' A submission run in IMMEDIATE mode.
' Traces to: FR-19..FR-28, FR-32..FR-36
'
' Two things that keep this short:
'  * Functional test results live in results.json, not the database.
'    The AI phase parses them back out. Phase 1 touches the database
'    only to record which targets still want AI feedback.
'  * ai_needed defaults to TRUE for every registered target. The
'    instructor's suite turns it OFF for targets that need no
'    commentary. There is nothing to "resolve" afterwards.
' =====================================================================

title Submission Run -- IMMEDIATE mode

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 220

actor Student as stud
participant "Gradescope" as gs #E8E8E8
box "Autograder Container" #F7F7F7
  participant "Autograder Script\n(run_autograder)" as script #FFF2CC
  participant "Instructor\nTest Suite" as ti #FFF2CC
  participant "Reporting\nLibrary" as wrap #FBE6D4
  participant "AI Grading\nPhase" as aip #D6E4F0
end box
participant "Web API" as api #DCEFD9
database "Grading DB" as db #DCEFD9
participant "LLM\nProviders" as llm #E8E8E8

stud -> gs : submit code
gs -> script : run_autograder

== Phase 1: functional testing ==

script -> ti : run the test suite
ti -> wrap : first call initialises the run
wrap -> api : POST /submissions\n{platform_submission_id, submitted_at}
api -> db : upsert submission,\ninsert submission_target per registered target\nwith ai_needed = TRUE
api --> wrap : 200 {submission_id}

note over api #FFF8DC
  **ai_needed defaults to ON.** Every registered
  target starts wanting feedback, and the suite
  opts targets out. Fail-open on cost: an
  instructor who never opts anything out pays
  for feedback on every target of every
  submission. Safer pedagogically, more
  expensive by default. Say so in the
  instructor guide.
end note

loop for each test the instructor wrote
  ti -> ti : run the assertion
  opt this target needs no AI commentary\n(e.g. the test passed)
    ti -> wrap : ai_not_needed(target)
    wrap -> api : POST .../ai-not-needed
    api -> db : UPDATE submission_target\nSET ai_needed = FALSE
    api --> wrap : 200
  end

  opt the test found something worth telling the model
    ti -> wrap : add_test_context(target, note)
    wrap -> api : POST .../test-context
    api -> db : INSERT test_context
    api --> wrap : 201
  end
end

note over ti #FFF8DC
  Both calls are optional and both name a target
  explicitly, so the identifier check covers
  them equally.

  add_test_context is where a diagnostic the
  test already computed gets carried into the
  prompt: the failing input, the expected and
  actual values, a hint about which branch was
  taken. The instructor decides what is worth
  saying, which is better than us guessing from
  raw output.
end note

note over wrap #FFF8DC
  **An unregistered target name fails loudly.**
  The API rejects a name not registered on this
  assignment with a 4xx naming it and listing
  the registered ones, and the library raises
  rather than swallowing it, so the instructor's
  test run fails. A typo that silently succeeds
  would mean feedback appearing on a target she
  meant to exclude, with nothing to explain it.
end note

ti --> script : suite exits

script -> gs : write results.json\n(functional test results)

note over script #FFF8DC
  **The defensive write.** Test results reach the
  student here, before any LLM is involved. If
  Phase 2 dies outright, this file is what they
  see. Nothing about the test run is stored in
  the database, so results.json is the only
  record of it, and the AI phase parses it back.
end note

== Phase 2: AI feedback ==

script -> aip : invoke AI phase
aip -> api : GET /submissions/{id}/ai-needed
api -> db : SELECT submission_target WHERE ai_needed,\njoin rubric WHERE is_current,\njoin test_context
api --> aip : 200 {targets[], rubric,\nfeedback_guidance,\ntest_context[]}

loop for each target with ai_needed
  aip -> aip : build prompt from the rubric,\nthe feedback guidance, the student's\ncode on disk, and any test_context

  note right of aip #FFF8DC
    Nothing is parsed out of results.json to get
    here. ai_needed said this target wants
    feedback, and test_context carries whatever
    diagnostic the instructor chose to pass us.
    The model locates the target in the source
    and reasons from the rubric.

    test_context is untrusted: assertion messages
    routinely embed the student's own output.
    Delimit it, label it as test output, cap its
    length, and keep the rubric authoritative
    over anything it says.
  end note

  group Multi-provider fan-out [FR-23, FR-27]
    par
      aip -> llm : Claude: generate
      llm --> aip : text, suggested_score
    else
      aip -> llm : ChatGPT: generate
      llm --> aip : text, suggested_score
    end
    aip -> aip : compare, estimate confidence,\nselect one
  end

  aip -> api : POST /feedback\n{target, responses[], comparison, selected}
  api -> db : insert feedback (one row per provider),\ninsert feedback_comparison,\nSET completed_at = now
  api --> aip : 201
end

alt feedback generated
  aip -> api : POST /submissions/{id}/ai-complete
  api -> db : UPDATE submission\nSET ai_graded_submitted_at = submitted_at\nWHERE every ai_needed row is done\nor out of attempts
  api --> aip : 200 {guard_set}
  aip -> aip : parse results.json and splice\nthe feedback onto the end

  note right of api #FFF8DC
    **The guard is set here, and only here in
    IMMEDIATE mode.** Without it, an instructor
    re-running the autograder to debug something
    would re-bill every target on the submission.

    Conditional on every ai_needed row being done
    or out of attempts, so a partial failure
    leaves it NULL and the state stays honest.
  end note

else failed after retries
  aip -> aip : append a note that AI feedback\ncould not be generated
  note right of aip #FFF8DC
    No deferral to the nightly run. IMMEDIATE
    means immediate: the student gets their test
    results plus an honest note, and that is the
    end of it. See the failure taxonomy below for
    what actually goes wrong.
  end note
end

aip -> gs : write the new results.json

note over aip, llm #FFF8DC
  **The model never touches results.json.**
  Its entire output is a string and a number.
  Parsing, inserting and writing the file are
  ordinary deterministic code in the AI phase.

  Do not be tempted to have the model emit the
  JSON, or a whole results.json, however
  convenient the prompt looks. One malformed or
  truncated response and the student's grade
  report is corrupt or empty, and the failure
  arrives silently on their screen rather than
  in a log.

  Treat the model's text as untrusted input on
  the way in as well: length-cap it, and escape
  it when inserting, since it lands in a file
  the platform renders.
end note
gs --> stud : test results, and either\nAI feedback or a note\nexplaining its absence

note over aip, llm #FFF8DC
  **Why Phase 2 fails, and whether retrying helps.**

  Transient, worth a retry inside this run:
   1. Rate limiting. Every submission for the
      assignment uses ONE instructor API key, and
      everyone submits in the last hour before the
      deadline. A burst of concurrent containers
      on one key makes 429 the expected case.
   2. Provider 5xx.
   3. Quota or billing exhausted mid-term.

  Not worth retrying:
   4. Context overflow on an enormous submission.
   5. Model refuses, or returns unparseable output.
   6. The container hits the platform's autograder
      timeout, in which case nothing runs at all
      and the defensive write is what survives.

  Note that 1 and 6 are CORRELATED WITH THE
  DEADLINE, so the failure rate is not uniform. It
  spikes exactly when the most students are
  affected. That is the strongest argument for
  preferring NIGHTLY in a large course, and an
  argument for serialising rather than fanning out
  the provider calls.
end note

@enduml
